    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d619">

                <pb facs="1786" n="1611" sameAs="frus:frus1961-63v10-12mSupp/pg1611"/>

                <head>619. Memorandum from McGeorge Bundy to General Taylor, February 19<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d619-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <date ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-head" calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1963-02-19">February 19, 1963</date>
                    </dateline></opener>
                <pb facs="d619-1" n="1" type="facsimile" xml:id="d619-1"/>
                <p>The President notes in a Cuban Task Force report on contingency planning for Cuba
                    that it would take 18 days from the moment of alert to make an invasion of the
                    island, and he told me to ask you for a comment on this delay and on the ways
                    and means which might be available for shortening it. His impression is that the
                    principal problem is shipping, but he would be glad to have an up-to-date
                    account from you.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed corresp="#p_BM_1">McGeorge Bundy</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d619-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d619-f1-content">
                <p>Transmits the President’s request for ways and means to shorten the 18-day lead
                    time in contingency planning for an invasion of Cuba. Top Secret. 1 p. Kennedy
                    Library, NSF, Countries Series, Cuba, General, vol. VII(B), 2/63.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
